+def flatten_batches_facts(order_rows, fills_rows, since: float) -> list:
+    """Every protective-flatten batch with an execution at or after `since`, costed as an EXIT. Pure.
+
+    ★★★ LED-02 (FX-EXEC2, 2026-09-13). The per-anchor table selects order rows by an anchors row's
+        `anchor_ts`, and a ladder flatten writes no anchors row — so the 2026-09-12 12:47Z flatten
+        (255 legs, 235,382.55 USDT) appeared NOWHERE in this summary, and the three-bucket rule, had it
+        been pointed at those rows, prices against `mid_at_anchor` (None by construction on a flatten)
+        and reads all 255 legs unpriced for ever. `cost_buckets.flatten_cost` prices each leg against its
+        own `mid_at_submit` and takes the fee from the fills ledger (E1 backfill), naming every leg it
+        cannot measure. A batch is in the window when any of its legs has a fill time (or, lacking one,
+        a row time) at or after `since`; `anchor_ts` on flatten rows is the row-WRITE time, after the
+        last fill (fact table NEW-01), so the fill time is the one read first.
